.TH SETURA 1 "" "Zyvor Setura" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
setura \- serve, publish and verify Gateway API routes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B setura
[\fB\-\-context\fR \fINAME\fR[,\fINAME\fR...] | \fB\-\-all\-contexts\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIflags\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B setura
forwards Kubernetes Gateway API listeners to IPv4 loopback and verifies
routes from the client side.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B explain URL
probe a URL from this host, stage by stage
.TP
.B check FILE [\-\-context a,b]
run a JSON file of route assertions
.TP
.B gate ROUTE URL [\-\-context a,b]
require current parent status and a probe streak
.TP
.B compare URL \-\-target dns|IP
probe the same URL via DNS and/or IPs
.TP
.B watch URL... [\-\-file FILE]
re\-probe on an interval; metrics, \-\-history, \-\-alert\-webhook/\-\-alert\-slack
.TP
.B history FILE [\-\-since 24h] [\-\-slo]
summarize watch history: uptime, p50/p95, last failure, regressions; \-\-slo for burn rates
.TP
.B slo [\-\-history FILE|\-\-admin ADDR] [\-\-config F]
availability and latency SLO burn rates (multi\-window) from probes or the gateway's metrics
.TP
.B why METHOD URL [\-\-config F|\-\-admin ADDR]
explain why the built\-in gateway routes a request the way it does
.TP
.B bench URL
repeat probes and report latency percentiles and budgets
.TP
.B load URL
keep\-alive load generator: requests/s, p50/p90/p99/p99.9, errors, gateway goroutines and memory
.TP
.B grpc\-health HOST:PORT
call grpc.health.v1.Health/Check over TLS (HTTP/2)
.TP
.B inspect ROUTE
show route conditions and backend inventory
.TP
.B listeners GATEWAY
show listener attachment and conditions
.TP
.B certs HOST:PORT
show and verify the presented TLS certificate chain
.TP
.B snapshot
capture Gateway and HTTPRoute specs
.TP
.B map
render the declared route topology
.TP
.B ui [\-\-listen ADDR]
live read\-only web dashboard: route map, lint, traffic, feed
.TP
.B connections [\-\-admin ADDR] [\-\-follow]
who connected to which listener, over what protocol, and where the gateway sent it
.TP
.B top [\-\-admin ADDR] [\-\-once]
live terminal view of requests/s, errors, p50/p95, ejected endpoints and connections
.TP
.B impact TARGET
query what a resource change would affect
.TP
.B lint
statically analyze specs (offline)
.TP
.B diff BEFORE AFTER
compare two snapshots
.TP
.B migrate check [\-\-ingress F] [\-\-routes F] ... | convert [\-\-from SRC] [PATH...] [\-\-out DIR]
compare Ingress rules with their converted routes (check); turn Ingress, Envoy Gateway, Traefik or nginx.conf into Gateway API YAML (convert)
.TP
.B replay \-\-log F \-\-before OLD \-\-after NEW
replay an access log through two gateway configs and report the routing impact
.TP
.B pr\-check \-\-base OLD \-\-head NEW \-\-traffic LOG
what share of recorded traffic a config change re\-routes, per route, with 404/403/429 and redirect changes; table, JSON or PR\-comment Markdown; \-\-fail\-if\-changed\-over PCT for CI
.TP
.B ai REPORT [\-\-model NAME]
explain saved evidence, optionally with local Ollama
.TP
.B gateway run|up|install|status|rollout|history|rollback|ai\-usage
built\-in Gateway API data plane, no Envoy needed; guarded config rollout; AI gateway routes (providers, token budgets, cache, PII) and `gateway ai\-usage`
.TP
.B shadow [\-\-admin ADDR] [\-\-follow] [\-\-fail\-under PCT]
compare mirrored HTTP and gRPC responses with the primary (built\-in gateway /shadow); exit 1 under the threshold
.TP
.B chaos run \-\-plan F | stop | status [\-\-admin ADDR]
game\-day fault injection (latency, abort, reset, truncate, throttle) with a safety guard on the gateway's own SLO signals; every fault expires, `chaos stop` kills them all
.TP
.B usage [invoice \-\-period YYYY\-MM] [\-\-by key|route|plan] [\-\-since 7d]
API usage per key, route or plan from the gateway's metering (table, JSON or CSV); invoice prices a month from the plan prices
.TP
.B contract learn|show|export|diff [\-\-learned F|\-\-admin ADDR] [\-\-spec openapi.json]
learn which endpoints the gateway serves; export an OpenAPI 3.1 document; find shadow, zombie and drifting APIs and auth gaps against a real spec (\-\-fail\-on for CI)
.TP
.B init [\-\-route host=upstream | \-\-openapi FILE]
scaffold a gateway config and checks.json from host=upstream pairs or an OpenAPI JSON file
.TP
.B publish GATEWAY \-\-listener NAME
forward an Envoy Gateway listener to IPv4 loopback
.TP
.B doctor [gateway]
check permissions needed by setura; `doctor gateway` checks a gateway config, ports, certificates and backends (\-\-kube adds CRDs and Gateway status)
.TP
.B capabilities
list GatewayClasses and controller features
.TP
.B exposure
show Service exposure
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
print a shell completion script
.TP
.B echo \-\-name NAME
run a tiny identity backend that answers with X\-Setura\-Backend
.TP
.B licence verify [KEY|\-\-file F] | show
verify a signed ZYV1 licence key offline (exit 0 valid, 1 invalid)
.TP
.B man
print the manual page (roff)
.TP
.B version [\-\-verbose]
print the version and build info
.PP
Run \fBsetura COMMAND \-h\fR for the flags of a command.
.SH MULTIPLE CONTEXTS
\fBcheck\fR, \fBsnapshot\fR, \fBgate\fR and \fBlint\fR accept a comma separated list of kube contexts, or \fB\-\-all\-contexts\fR, and merge the results with a per\-context field. The exit status is unchanged.
.SH ALERTING
\fBsetura watch\fR can post failure and recovery transitions with \fB\-\-alert\-webhook\fR and \fB\-\-alert\-slack\fR. The URLs are secrets and are never printed. \fBsetura history\fR summarizes the file written by \fB\-\-history\fR.
.SH ROUTING DECISIONS
\fBsetura why\fR METHOD URL explains which listener, route, rule and backend the built\-in gateway picks for a request and why every other candidate lost. It evaluates a gateway config (\fB\-\-config\fR, \fB\-\-snapshot\fR) or asks a running gateway (\fB\-\-admin\fR ADDR, endpoint /why) and sends no traffic. \fB\-H\fR adds request headers, \fB\-\-grpc\fR service/method explains a gRPC call, \fB\-\-sni\fR host a TLSRoute connection, \fB\-\-format\fR json prints the trace. It also shows the trace headers and X\-Forwarded\-For the backend receives, the route's retry policy, the rate\-limit bucket (\fB\-\-rate\-limit\fR, \fB\-\-rate\-burst\fR, \fB\-\-rate\-key\fR model the gateway's policy offline, \fB\-\-admin\fR adds the live bucket) and mirrors with shadow\-diff, and a file route's metadata.creationTimestamp breaks ties. It exits 0 when routed, 1 when no route matches and 2 on a usage error.
.PP
\fBsetura replay\fR reads the gateway's JSON and text access logs and Common or Combined Log Format (\fB\-\-default\-host\fR supplies the Host those lack). Besides route, backend, outcome and weight changes it reports retry, timeout, mirror and rate\-limit policy changes (\fB\-\-before\-rate\-limit\fR, \fB\-\-after\-rate\-limit\fR), flags backends that fail in the log, and replays gRPC calls and TLS passthrough connections through GRPCRoutes and TLSRoutes.
.SH GATEWAY TRAFFIC SECURITY
The built\-in gateway can protect an HTTPRoute rule with an \fBsetura.zyvor.dev/auth\fR block (JWT, API key, basic auth, client address lists, required client certificate), validate client certificates on HTTPS listeners (Gateway API frontend TLS validation) and staple OCSP responses (\fB\-\-ocsp\-staple\fR). \fBsetura gateway hash\fR \fB\-\-kind\fR apikey|password reads a secret on stdin and prints the hash to store. \fB\-\-admin\-token\fR TOKEN (or SETURA_ADMIN_TOKEN) protects the admin endpoint and is required to bind it off loopback; \fB\-\-admin\-open\-ready\fR leaves /ready open. \fBsetura why\fR shows whether the credentials given with \fB\-H\fR would pass. Credentials are redacted everywhere. See docs/security.md.
.PP
GRPCRoute rules take the same auth block; rejections are Trailers\-Only gRPC statuses (UNAUTHENTICATED, PERMISSION_DENIED, RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED). \fB\-\-client\-allow\-san\fR and \fB\-\-client\-allow\-cn\fR (or a Gateway's \fBsetura.zyvor.dev/client\-allow\fR annotation) restrict validated client certificates to named SANs or subject CNs, exact or *.suffix; refusals are logged and counted. \fB\-\-client\-cert\-xfcc\fR adds an Envoy\-style X\-Forwarded\-Client\-Cert header. A RequestHeaderModifier value may use {client_ip}, {request_id}, {host}, {scheme} and {listener}. \fB\-\-access\-log\-exclude\fR PATTERN and \fB\-\-access\-log\-sample\fR N keep requests out of the access log while they still count in metrics. See docs/client\-cert\-authz.md and docs/headers\-and\-logs.md.
.SH GATEWAY LIMITS AND RESPONSES
A rule can answer without a backend with a \fBsetura.zyvor.dev/direct\-response\fR block (status, body, headers), carry its own token bucket with \fBsetura.zyvor.dev/rate\-limit\fR (rate, burst, key route|client\-ip|header:NAME; the gateway\-wide \fB\-\-rate\-limit\fR still applies) and override the gateway limits with \fBsetura.zyvor.dev/limits\fR. \fBsetura gateway run\fR limits requests with \fB\-\-max\-request\-body\fR (413), \fB\-\-max\-header\-bytes\fR (431), \fB\-\-header\-read\-timeout\fR, \fB\-\-idle\-timeout\fR and \fB\-\-stream\-idle\-timeout\fR, normalises paths before routing with \fB\-\-normalize\-path\fR, \fB\-\-merge\-slashes\fR and \fB\-\-reject\-escaped\-slashes\fR (all off by default) and bounds TLS with \fB\-\-tls\-min\-version\fR and \fB\-\-tls\-max\-version\fR. See docs/direct\-response.md and docs/limits.md.
.SH API PLANS AND USAGE
\fBsetura gateway run \-\-plans\fR FILE turns on API monetisation in the built\-in gateway: a hot\-reloaded JSON plan file defines named plans (requests per minute, day and month, a body byte quota, burst, allowed routes and methods, an optional price per 1000 requests, hard or soft enforcement) and maps API keys, JWT subjects, tenants or a trusted upstream header to a plan. Requests over a hard quota get 429 with Retry\-After, a JSON body naming the quota and X\-Quota\-Limit, X\-Quota\-Remaining and X\-Quota\-Reset headers. Usage is metered per identity, route and plan, bounded in memory and persisted atomically with \fB\-\-usage\-state\fR. \fBsetura usage\fR prints it (\fB\-\-by\fR key|route|plan|day, \fB\-\-since\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR table|json|csv); \fBsetura usage invoice \-\-period\fR YYYY\-MM prices a month. \fBsetura why \-\-plans\fR FILE explains which plan and quota apply to a request and \fBsetura lint \-\-plans\fR FILE checks the file against the routes (SET020 to SET022). The gateway admin endpoint serves /usage, /usage.csv and /plans. Counters and quotas are per gateway replica: nothing is shared between replicas; see docs/metering-plans.md.
.SH GATEWAY TRAFFIC SECURITY
The built\-in gateway can protect an HTTPRoute rule with an \fBsetura.zyvor.dev/auth\fR block (JWT, API key, basic auth, client address lists, required client certificate), validate client certificates on HTTPS listeners (Gateway API frontend TLS validation) and staple OCSP responses (\fB\-\-ocsp\-staple\fR). \fBsetura gateway hash\fR \fB\-\-kind\fR apikey|password reads a secret on stdin and prints the hash to store. \fB\-\-admin\-token\fR TOKEN (or SETURA_ADMIN_TOKEN) protects the admin endpoint and is required to bind it off loopback; \fB\-\-admin\-open\-ready\fR leaves /ready open. \fBsetura why\fR shows whether the credentials given with \fB\-H\fR would pass. Credentials are redacted everywhere. See docs/security.md.
.SH AI GATEWAY
A rule with a \fBsetura.zyvor.dev/ai\fR block (a rule field, or the route annotation of that name, or \fBsetura.zyvor.dev/ai.rule\-N\fR) serves OpenAI\-compatible \fB/v1/chat/completions\fR, \fB/v1/completions\fR and \fB/v1/embeddings\fR and Anthropic \fB/v1/messages\fR requests from a list of providers with weights and priorities. Provider API keys come from the environment, a file or a Kubernetes Secret and replace the client credential. The gateway counts prompt and completion tokens from the responses (JSON or the last SSE event) and reports them per route, provider, model and key in /metrics and in the access log (tokens_in, tokens_out, provider, model, cache_hit, cost_estimate). Token budgets per API key, JWT claim or tenant header (per minute, per day, per month) answer 429 with Retry\-After before a request is forwarded. A 5xx, 429 or timeout moves the request to the next provider; the existing outlier ejection takes a failing provider out of rotation. An opt\-in exact\-match prompt cache and PII redaction (\fBredact\fR, \fBblock\fR or \fBreport\fR) are per route. \fB\-\-ai\-prices\fR FILE supplies the price table for cost estimates and \fB\-\-ai\-max\-key\-labels\fR N bounds metric label cardinality. \fBsetura gateway ai\-usage\fR [\fB\-\-admin\fR ADDR] [\fB\-\-route\fR R] [\fB\-\-key\fR K] prints usage, budgets and provider health from the admin /ai/usage endpoint, and \fBsetura why\fR explains which provider a request would use and why. Lint rules SET040 to SET047 cover misconfigurations. See docs/ai\-gateway.md.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success
.TP
.B 1
the check, gate, lint, replay or migrate check failed or found differences, or why found no route
.TP
.B 2
usage or input error
.SH GATEWAY ROLLOUT
\fBsetura gateway rollout\fR lints a candidate snapshot, backs up the live file in \fI.setura\-history\fR, replaces it atomically, waits for the admin /status generation, runs a checks file for an observation window and restores the backup on any failure. The window is judged on the probes and on the gateway's own /stats and /metrics deltas (\fB\-\-max\-error\-rate\fR and \fB\-\-max\-p95\fR apply to both). A candidate that changes canary weights is applied in stages 10,50,100 unless \fB\-\-steps\fR or \fB\-\-no\-steps\fR is given. A rollout lock left by a killed rollout (dead process on this host, or a heartbeat older than \fB\-\-lock\-timeout\fR) is taken over; a live owner never is. Relative certificate paths resolve against the candidate's directory. \fBsetura gateway history\fR lists the backups and \fBsetura gateway rollback\fR restores one.
.SH GATEWAY INSTALL AND CLUSTER STATUS
\fBsetura gateway install\fR prints plain YAML for the in\-cluster controller and never applies it. \fB\-\-service\-type\fR ClusterIP|NodePort|LoadBalancer, \fB\-\-node\-port\fR HTTP=PORT,HTTPS=PORT, \fB\-\-listener\-port\fR N (repeatable), \fB\-\-external\-traffic\-policy\fR, \fB\-\-host\-network\fR, \fB\-\-replicas\fR, \fB\-\-arg\fR FLAG (repeatable), \fB\-\-resources\fR, \fB\-\-node\-selector\fR and \fB\-\-expose\-gateway\-ports\fR remove the need to patch the output. The admin token Secret is not printed unless \fB\-\-create\-admin\-secret\fR is given, so re\-applying never rotates the token; \fB\-\-admin\-token\-secret\fR NAME names an existing Secret. \fB\-\-crds\fR with \fB\-\-gateway\-api\-version\fR prints the command that installs the Gateway API CRDs. \fBsetura gateway install \-\-check\fR and \fBsetura doctor gateway \-\-kube\fR report missing or too old CRDs and which Setura feature needs which release. \fBsetura gateway status\fR lists Gateways and routes with Accepted, Programmed and ResolvedRefs and the reason. See docs/install\-options.md and docs/cluster\-troubleshooting.md.
.SH ENVOY GATEWAY COMPATIBILITY
\fBsetura gateway run \-\-kubernetes \-\-envoy\-compat\fR also reads Envoy Gateway's optional policy CRDs (BackendTrafficPolicy, ClientTrafficPolicy, SecurityPolicy, HTTPRouteFilter and Backend in gateway.envoyproxy.io/v1alpha1; absent CRDs are fine) and translates what Setura can express: retry, timeout, circuit breaker, active and passive health checks, local rate limit, fault injection, mirroring, TLS version and client validation, JWT, API key, ExternalAuth, CORS, IP authorization, PROXY protocol to backends, direct responses and Backend endpoints. Targets (Gateway, listener, HTTPRoute, GRPCRoute, rule) follow Envoy Gateway precedence: the most specific wins, and mergeType StrategicMerge or JSONMerge merges over the less specific policy. Each policy gets Accepted, Conflicted and Overridden conditions; the reason Unsupported lists the fields Setura ignores, and a guard it cannot enforce (basicAuth, oidc) refuses the targeted traffic. \fBsetura gateway install \-\-envoy\-compat\fR grants read and status access to those CRDs only. \fBsetura migrate convert \-\-from envoy\-gateway\fR shares the translation, and \fB\-\-compat\-report\fR lists every field with Supported, Partial or Unsupported. \fBsetura gateway status\fR and \fBsetura doctor gateway \-\-kube\fR list the policies and ignored fields; lint rules SET080 to SET083. See docs/envoy\-compat.md and docs/envoy\-gateway\-compat.md.
.SH LICENCE
\fBsetura licence verify\fR [KEY | \fB\-\-file\fR F] checks a signed ZYV1 licence key offline against the public keys embedded in the binary and prints the tier, cluster limit, expiry, licensee and status (valid, expired, bad signature, wrong product). \fBsetura licence show\fR checks the key in SETURA_LICENCE_KEY or the file named by SETURA_LICENCE_FILE (default: setura/licence.key in the user config directory). It exits 0 for a valid key, 1 for an invalid one and 2 on a usage error. Setura works without a key; \fBsetura version \-\-verbose\fR only reports the status and never blocks a command.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/zyvorai/zyvor\-setura\-gateway
